  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nAn exceptionally quiet morning is ongoing across the area as a\nweak cold front pushed through, with winds turning westerly to\nnorthwesterly with clearing skies due to weak subsidence via \nCAA. There is a bit of patchy fog within central MN, however it \nis not widespread and we do not expect significant impacts as a \nresult, with fog quick to burn off once the sun rises in a few \nhours. Temperatures will remain a few degrees above normal today\nwith highs in the low to mid 50s with weak surface high \npressure centered south of the region keeping skies mostly \nclear. A few clouds return tonight as yet another weak Canadian \nlow pressure system slides west to east across our neighbor to \nthe north, with a similar situation to what we just had as a \nweak series of frontal boundaries will move through within 12 \nhours of each other between Monday evening and Tuesday morning. \nNo rain showers are expected with the main result being a brief \nincrease in cloud cover overnight, with weak high pressure once \nagain sliding in afterwards on Tuesday. \n\nA ridge building in the western CONUS will amplify the northerly \nflow aloft on Wednesday which will also begin to spin up a weak \nsurface low tracking from roughly Nebraska to Illinois during \nthe day as 500mb heights fall with northerly flow aloft. The \nheight fall plus CAA could squeeze out a few showers mainly \nWednesday morning as the surface low moves south of the area, \nhowever the lack of moisture should mean any activity is limited\nboth in duration and intensity, with only a few hundredths of \nan inch at best from any showers. As the surface low moves away \nfrom the area and heights begin to steady later in the day, we \nshould dry out quickly with cloud cover also retreating amidst \nhigh pressure building on the eastern side of the ridge which \nhas yet to really move from the western CONUS. The position of \nthe ridge and high pressure will result in some cooler \ntemperatures from Wednesday through Friday with another chance \nfor some sub-freezing lows, mainly on Friday as highs drop into \nthe low to mid 40s. The high pressure then begins to move \ntowards the southeastern CONUS with the ridge beginning to \nflatten, raising 500mb heights and once again bringing \ntemperatures back above normal, in addition to keeping us dry. \nThis longwave ridge spanning the entire CONUS looks to remain in\nplace for at least the first half of next week, with any \npotential pattern shift waiting until the latter half of next \nweek. Long range ensemble guidance keeps the weather quiet until\nthen, however once the ridge moves eastwards we could see \nbetter chances for precipitation return across the area, but any\nmajor systems are too low confidence to truly take note of for \nnow.",
